&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt; &lt;h1&gt; &lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+Source+Specific+Multicast+for+Direct+Sockets+API%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Intent to Ship: Source Specific Multicast for Direct Sockets API&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/h1&gt; &lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt; &lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Chris+Harrelson%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Chris Harrelson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261021" rel="nofollow"&gt;Wed, 21 Oct 2026 09:38:01 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;LGTM2
On Wed, Oct 21, 2026 at 8:19 AM Alex Russell &lt;&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="691a05000e011d0510474747290a011b0604001c0447061b0e"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;
&gt; Thanks Omar, &gt; &gt; LGTM1 &gt; &gt; On Tuesday, October 20, 2026 at 6:28:37 AM UTC-7 &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="214e4c0f0f0f61434d4e424a424052550f4f4455"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; &gt; wrote: &gt; &gt;&gt; Hi Alex, &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; The benefit is that SSM makes the multicast API usable beyond a single &gt;&gt; LAN. Multicast that crosses network boundaries is SSM in practice: &gt;&gt; - RFC 8815 deprecates inter-domain ASM. &gt;&gt; - ISP and enterprise deployments carry channels in the SSM ranges (232/8, &gt;&gt; FF3x::/32), where routers only forward source-specific joins (RFC 4607). &gt;&gt; - AMT relays (RFC 7450) serve (S,G) channels. &gt;&gt; That is the TreeDN model (RFC 9706): native multicast where the network &gt;&gt; supports it and AMT relays everywhere else. Lenny Giuliano's introduction: &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://community.juniper.net/blogs/lenny/2025/08/21/introduction-to-TreeDN"&gt;https://community.juniper.net/blogs/lenny/2025/08/21/introduction-to-TreeDN&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; An ASM-only IWA can't receive those channels off its LAN. Its only &gt;&gt; workaround is an AMT gateway written in JS over unicast UDP, which needs a &gt;&gt; relay and gives up native delivery on networks that support it. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; Who's asking: &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; 1. My company, Blockcast. We built that workaround, and this intent lets &gt;&gt; us drop it on native networks. &gt;&gt; - Our Chrome extension, Multicast Gateway (BEACON), gives pages a small &gt;&gt; window.multicast API: &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/multicast-gateway-beacon/djikmiecejmojgcglkfmihhkfgekjlaj"&gt;https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/multicast-gateway-beacon/djikmiecejmojgcglkfmihhkfgekjlaj&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; - The sockets live in our IWA, which uses Direct Sockets and only serves &gt;&gt; sites the user has allowed (an exact-origin allowlist). Pages get multicast &gt;&gt; only through components the user installed: &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; const sub = await window.multicast.subscribe({ &gt;&gt; group: '232.1.1.50', port: 8000, &gt;&gt; source: '192.0.2.10', // SSM; omit for ASM &gt;&gt; consume: 'bytes', &gt;&gt; }); &gt;&gt; sub.addEventListener('bytes', (e) =&gt; handle(e.detail)); // one &gt;&gt; ArrayBuffer per datagram &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; - Each subscription is delivered natively or through an AMT relay. The &gt;&gt; native SSM case is a direct call to MulticastController.joinGroup(group, { &gt;&gt; sourceAddress }), which is what this intent ships. Outside the dev trial &gt;&gt; that option is dropped and the join silently becomes any-source, so today &gt;&gt; our users beyond the LAN need the AMT path. &gt;&gt; - Public demo: &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://blockcast.network/live"&gt;https://blockcast.network/live&lt;/a&gt; compares a live ISS camera &gt;&gt; feed in the browser over QUIC, ROUTE, AMT and native multicast. The AMT and &gt;&gt; native multicast choices appear once the extension and IWA are installed. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; 2. The IETF MBONED community. Browser multicast drew a lot of interest at &gt;&gt; IETF 126 in July (minutes: &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/minutes-126-mboned-202607211430/"&gt;https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/minutes-126-mboned-202607211430/&lt;/a&gt;): &gt;&gt; - QUICast (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://quicast.de/"&gt;https://quicast.de/&lt;/a&gt;) showed a modified Firefox receiving &gt;&gt; multicast natively beneath WebTransport, without an IWA. &gt;&gt; - Aircast reported live-event deployments over multicast and AMT, &gt;&gt; including Wimbledon. &gt;&gt; - Chris Needham of the BBC and W3C board of directors offered to help &gt;&gt; coordinate a browser multicast API in W3C. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; 3. Other signals: &gt;&gt; - On the proposal (#80), Harald Alvestrand recommended SSM as the only &gt;&gt; safe model beyond the local network. &gt;&gt; - Node.js dgram already exposes the same capability &gt;&gt; (addSourceSpecificMembership). &gt;&gt; - Jake Holland's Multicast Receiver API explainer is SSM-only for the &gt;&gt; same reason: &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/GrumpyOldTroll/wicg-multicast-receiver-api/blob/master/explainer.md"&gt;https://github.com/GrumpyOldTroll/wicg-multicast-receiver-api/blob/master/explainer.md&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; Outside this community, the Ready for Trial thread got no replies. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; A larger discussion with W3C about multicast on the web is still needed, &gt;&gt; including how IWAs will roll out: today the install UX isn't great and &gt;&gt; there's no mobile support. But this is an exciting first step, and a small &gt;&gt; one: an optional sourceAddress on joinGroup()/leaveGroup(), mapped to &gt;&gt; MCAST_JOIN_SOURCE_GROUP, with no new permission or prompt. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; Thanks, &gt;&gt; Omar &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; On Tue, Oct 20, 2026 at 1:48 AM Alex Russell &lt;&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="66150a0f010e120a1f48484826050e14090b0f130b48091401"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; wrote: &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; What's the developer benefit for SSM? Is anyone asking for this? &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; Best, &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; Alex &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; On Monday, October 19, 2026 at 1:57:36 PM UTC-7 Reilly Grant wrote: &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; IWA OWNER LGTM &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This is an extension to an extension of an existing IWA-only API. The &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; previous justification for why multicast would create a privacy issues for &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the web platform (see also threads on net-dev@) still applies. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Reilly Grant | Software Engineer | &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="314354585d1f1f1f715259435e5c58445c1f5e4356"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; | Google Chrome &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.google.com/chrome"&gt;https://www.google.com/chrome&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; On Wed, Oct 14, 2026 at 7:43 PM Chromestatus &lt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="5130357f7f7f1132237c2225302524227f30212122213e253c30383d7f323e3c"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Contact emails* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="84ebaaaaaac4e6e8ebe7efe7e5f7f0aaeae1f0"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Explainer* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/blob/main/docs/multicast-explainer.md"&gt;https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/blob/main/docs/multicast-explainer.md&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Specification* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://wicg.github.io/direct-sockets/#dom-multicastgroupoptions-sourceaddress"&gt;https://wicg.github.io/direct-sockets/#dom-multicastgroupoptions-sourceaddress&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Summary* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Adds Source-Specific Multicast (SSM) support to the Direct Sockets &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; API, allowing Isolated Web Apps to optionally specify a source address &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; when &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; joining multicast groups to receive UDP packets only from that source. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Without a source address, joinGroup() uses Any-Source Multicast (ASM), &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; which receives packets from any source sending to the group. SSM is &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; essential to using multicast outside of private networks, simplifying &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; packet routing, and filtering traffic at the network level by source &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; address, which mitigates spoofing and denial-of-service attacks. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Blink component* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Blink&gt;Network-&gt;Direct Sockets &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork-%3EDirect%20Sockets%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork-%3EDirect%20Sockets%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Missing feature &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Motivation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; RFC 8815 deprecates ASM for inter-domain use due to security &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; vulnerabilities and operational complexity. Without SSM support, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; developers &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; building multicast applications in Isolated Web Apps face several &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; problems: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Limited deployment scenarios: Applications are restricted to controlled &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; private networks where all sources are trusted, preventing use cases like &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; internet-wide content distribution or cross-organization IoT systems. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Application-layer filtering overhead: Applications must receive and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; process &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; all packets sent to a multicast group before validating the source, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; wasting &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; CPU cycles and bandwidth on potentially malicious traffic. SSM is the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; IETF-recommended model for multicast across domains (RFC 4607, RFC 8815) &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and is supported by many enterprise and ISP networks. Adding SSM support &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; enables more secure multicast communication beyond private network &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; boundaries. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Initial public proposal* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/issues/80"&gt;https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/issues/80&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; TAG does not review Isolated Web Apps: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/842#issuecomment-2917031448"&gt;https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/842#issuecomment-2917031448&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; API design was reviewed on WICG/direct-sockets#80 and #81 (approved by &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; reillyeon and GrapeGreen). Alternatives considered there: a positional &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; source argument (replaced by an options dictionary) and a &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; source-filter-list API like IP_MSFILTER (rejected because each call &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; replaces the whole list). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review status* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Not applicable &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Risks* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Low. sourceAddress is an optional dictionary member: code that doesn't &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; pass it behaves exactly as before, and joinedGroups still returns strings &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; for ASM memberships. Where SSM is unavailable (older Chrome, or the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; feature &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; turned off), sourceAddress is ignored and joinGroup() makes an ASM join; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; apps can detect this because joinedGroups then lists a string, not a &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; MulticastMembership. Other browsers may choose to implement this API. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Gecko*: No signal Mozilla's position on the parent Direct Sockets &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; API is negative: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/431"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/431&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebKit*: No signal The WebKit position request for Isolated Web Apps &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; is open without a position: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/184"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/184&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web developers*: No signals The Ready for Trial thread got no &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; replies. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Other signals*: Harald Alvestrand recommended SSM in &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/issues/80:"&gt;https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/issues/80:&lt;/a&gt; &quot;Basically &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Any-Source Multicast with a scope larger than your local network is a &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; high-powered DDOS vector, not something that it's safe to build services &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; on, so I heartily recommend adding support for Single-Source Multicast.&quot; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Node.js dgram has the same capability (addSourceSpecificMembership / &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; dropSourceSpecificMembership). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Ergonomics* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The API follows the OS socket API (MCAST_JOIN_SOURCE_GROUP) and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Node.js dgram. Each (group, source) pair is its own membership, several &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; sources for one group are additive (RFC 4604), and a socket cannot mix ASM &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and SSM memberships for the same group. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Activation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Receiving SSM traffic needs a network that supports IGMPv3/MLDv2 and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; routes SSM, or an AMT (RFC 7450) gateway on the host or local router that &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; tunnels it from a relay. Apps can't polyfill native SSM reception; the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; closest substitute is running their own AMT gateway over unicast UDP. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Security* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SSM is part of the Direct Sockets API, and its security and privacy &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; self-review applies: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/blob/main/docs/security-privacy-self-review.md"&gt;https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/blob/main/docs/security-privacy-self-review.md&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SSM-specific points: - Exposure is the same as ASM multicast: Isolated Web &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Apps with the &quot;direct-sockets-multicast&quot; permissions policy, on bound UDP &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; sockets that passed the local-network and loopback-network permission &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; checks. Since M150, ChromeOS users can also install IWAs that are on &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Chrome's IWA allowlist; a user-installed IWA gets that policy only if the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; allowlist grants it the DIRECT_SOCKETS_MULTICAST entitlement. There is no &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; new permission, prompt or content setting. - It reduces injection risk &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; compared with ASM: the OS and SSM routers drop datagrams from other source &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; addresses, so off-path hosts cannot inject traffic (RFC 8815 §3.2.3). It &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; is &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; not authentication: a host that forges the source address can potentially &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; still inject (RFC 4607 §7.4), so apps that need authenticity must &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; authenticate content, as with ASM. That also covers the fallback to an ASM &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; join when SSM is unavailable (see Interoperability). - The Linux &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; network-service sandbox allows setsockopt MCAST_JOIN_SOURCE_GROUP / &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; MCAST_LEAVE_SOURCE_GROUP (crrev.com/c/7641869). No other sandbox &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; change. - The network service re-checks the renderer's arguments but &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; missed &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; one case, which predates SSM: a compromised renderer of an IWA allowed to &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; use multicast could crash the network service (a NOTREACHED or CHECK, i.e. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; denial of service) with an empty group address. crrev.com/c/8583003 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; (RestrictedUDPSocket rejects non-multicast groups with a bad message) and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; crrev.com/c/8583123 (network::UDPSocket rejects invalid group &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; addresses for all clients) fix it and land before the flag flips. - An SSM &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; join sends an IGMPv3/MLDv2 report that names the (source, group) pair on &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the local link, as any native SSM receiver does. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebView application risks* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; that it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No. Direct Sockets is not available in WebView. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Debuggability* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DevTools' Network panel lists Direct Sockets UDP sockets, and the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; experimental CDP events Network.directUDPSocketJoinedMulticastGroup / &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; directUDPSocketLeftMulticastGroup report the groups a socket joins and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; leaves. For SSM, their IPAddress field currently carries &quot;group@source&quot; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; (e.g. &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="b88a8b8a968996899689f889818a9688968a96898888"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;); a follow-up will add a separate &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; sourceAddress parameter (crbug.com/461262401). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; MulticastController.joinedGroups returns {groupAddress, sourceAddress} for &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SSM memberships, and invalid input throws specific TypeError or &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; InvalidStateError messages. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Mac, Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; It is implemented on desktop platforms but only available to end users &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; where Isolated Web Apps are supported, which is currently ChromeOS. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Android &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and WebView do not support Direct Sockets. Known macOS issue: on the macOS &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; 15 test bots, joining a group with a second source fails with NetworkError &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; for a reason not yet understood (crbug.com/443716695). It succeeds on &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; macOS 26, and single-source joins succeed on both. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; There is no SSM WPT yet (follow-up tracked in crbug.com/461262401): &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the spec rejects loopback sources, so the test has to send from a &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; non-loopback address of the test host, which I haven't validated on the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WPT &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; bots yet. The ASM multicast WPT &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; (direct-sockets/udp_socket_multicast.https.html) runs in Chromium's &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; virtual/direct-sockets suite (Timeout on Mac, crbug.com/443716695; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; flaky on Linux, crbug.com/570251397). No Chromium test sends SSM &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; datagrams yet either; adding one is tracked in crbug.com/461262401. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SSM is covered by: - blink_unittests: MulticastControllerSsmTest.* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; (validation, membership rules, IP-version mismatch, ASM/SSM mixing) and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; IDL feature-flag tests - content_browsertests: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DirectSocketsBoundUdpBrowserTest.*SSM* - browser_tests (installed IWA): &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; ChromeDirectSocketsUdpIsolatedWebAppMulticastTest.*SSM* - net_unittests: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; UDPSocketTest.JoinSourceGroup*, LeaveSourceGroup*, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SSMSourceFilteringMultiNIC* (join and leave with two sources) - &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; services_unittests: the bad-message and invalid-address tests in &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; crrev.com/c/8583003 and crrev.com/c/8583123 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *DevTrial instructions* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/blob/main/docs/multicast-explainer.md#receiving-datagrams-example-source-specific-multicast"&gt;https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/blob/main/docs/multicast-explainer.md#receiving-datagrams-example-source-specific-multicast&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Flag name on about://flags* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; source-specific-multicast-in-direct-sockets &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Finch feature name* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SourceSpecificMulticastInDirectSockets &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Rollout plan* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Will ship enabled for all users &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; False &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Tracking bug* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/461262401"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/461262401&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Measurement* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The existing MulticastController use counters &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; (MulticastControllerJoinGroupFunction, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; MulticastControllerLeaveGroupFunction, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; MulticastControllerJoinedGroupsAttribute) count ASM and SSM calls &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; together. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; A new use counter, MulticastControllerJoinGroupWithSourceAddress, counts &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SSM joins and lands before the flip (crrev.com/c/8574609). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Availability expectation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Available only to Isolated Web Apps, on platforms where IWAs are &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; supported (currently ChromeOS): IWAs installed by an admin or in developer &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; mode, and user-installed IWAs that Chrome's IWA allowlist grants the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DIRECT_SOCKETS_MULTICAST entitlement. Not available on Android or WebView. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Adoption expectation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Initially a small number of IWA developers receiving multicast from &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; known sources, for example live streaming over SSM-enabled networks or AMT &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; relays. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Estimated milestones* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on desktop 160 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DevTrial on desktop 152 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Anticipated spec changes* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; of &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None expected. crrev.com/c/8582708 (lands before the flip) makes &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Chromium reject loopback sourceAddress values, as the spec already &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; requires. Two Chromium behaviors are not in the spec text yet: a socket &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; cannot mix ASM and SSM memberships for one group, and leaveGroup() &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; validates its arguments like joinGroup(). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/pull/86"&gt;https://github.com/WICG/direct-sockets/pull/86&lt;/a&gt; adds both. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/6208452397498368?gate=6516721057923072"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/6208452397498368?gate=6516721057923072&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Links to previous Intent discussions* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Intent to Prototype: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/692dffe2.050a0220.17ec37.0673.GAE%40google.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/692dffe2.050a0220.17ec37.0673.GAE%40google.com&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Ready for Trial: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/blink-dev/c/vMmLZVd7rQI"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/g/blink-dev/c/vMmLZVd7rQI&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com"&gt;https://chromestatus.com&lt;/a&gt;&gt;. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; -- &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Groups &quot;blink-dev&quot; group. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; an email to &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="a6c4cacfc8cd8bc2c3d08dd3c8d5d3c4d5c5d4888888e6c5ced4c9cbcfd3cb88c9d4c1"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; To view this discussion visit &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6ad03dc5.32cf16a2.aa5e8.002a.GAE%40google.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6ad03dc5.32cf16a2.aa5e8.002a.GAE%40google.com&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6ad03dc5.32cf16a2.aa5e8.002a.GAE%40google.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6ad03dc5.32cf16a2.aa5e8.002a.GAE%40google.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; . &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; -- &gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &gt; &quot;blink-dev&quot; group. &gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an &gt; email to &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="a9cbc5c0c7c284cdccdf82dcc7dadccbdacadb878787e9cac1dbc6c4c0dcc487c6dbce"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;. &gt; To view this discussion visit &gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/77558f26-eaab-4a14-b5f4-ad75f948f1f6n%40chromium.org"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/77558f26-eaab-4a14-b5f4-ad75f948f1f6n%40chromium.org&lt;/a&gt; &gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/77558f26-eaab-4a14-b5f4-ad75f948f1f6n%40chromium.org?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/77558f26-eaab-4a14-b5f4-ad75f948f1f6n%40chromium.org?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt; . &gt;
-- You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;blink-dev&quot; group. To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="c9aba5a0a7a2e4adacbfe2bca7babcabbaaabbe7e7e789aaa1bba6a4a0bca4e7a6bbae"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;. To view this discussion visit &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/CAOMQ%2Bw_F4V%3DQmMytduzNor5TgytJS3i8P%3D_sqPHSx8HskndFqA%40mail.gmail.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/CAOMQ%2Bw_F4V%3DQmMytduzNor5TgytJS3i8P%3D_sqPHSx8HskndFqA%40mail.gmail.com&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;/pre&gt;
