&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+Web+Bluetooth%5C%3A+BluetoothRemoteGATTServer.maxWriteWithoutResponseSize%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Intent to Ship: Web Bluetooth: BluetoothRemoteGATTServer.maxWriteWithoutResponseSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Alex+Russell%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Alex Russell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261027" rel="nofollow"&gt;Tue, 27 Oct 2026 11:57:04 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;LGTM1 on the condition that tests land soon; not a ship blocker, but within 
the next month would be best as others are looking at implementing WebBT 
and tests are the best guide.&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

Best,

Alex

On Wednesday, October 21, 2026 at 4:24:35 PM UTC-7 &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="e8808289869dc6c6c6a88f85898184c68b8785"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; wrote:

&gt; Please file a Mozilla standards request for this feature.
&gt;
&gt; Filed: &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1473"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1473&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; will update feature entry as soon theres a response.
&gt;
&gt; Could a WPT be added for this?
&gt;
&gt; Not today. Web Bluetooth WPTs drive fake peripherals through the bluetooth 
&gt; BiDi module in the spec’s Automated testing 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://webbluetoothcg.github.io/web-bluetooth/#automated-testing"&gt;https://webbluetoothcg.github.io/web-bluetooth/#automated-testing&lt;/a&gt;&gt; 
&gt; section, and neither those bluetooth.simulate* commands nor Chromium’s 
&gt; FakeCentral/FakePeripheral know anything about ATT_MTU. A fake device 
&gt; therefore always sits at the default, so there is nothing a test could 
&gt; assert.
&gt;
&gt; Making it testable means an mtu on the GATT connection response plus a 
&gt; simulateGattMtuChange command, specced in that Automated testing section 
&gt; and implemented in the Chromium emulation layer. Happy to do all of it. How 
&gt; would you like it, land the automation commands in the spec first and have 
&gt; the WPTs as a shipping blocker, or ship on the current unit test coverage (
&gt; web_bluetooth_service_impl_unittest.cc, bluetooth_gatt_bluez_unittest.cc, 
&gt; global-interface-listing) and track the WPTs as a follow-up?
&gt;
&gt;
&gt; cheers,
&gt; helmut
&gt; ​
&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="d7b3b6b9f9f9f997babeb4a5b8a4b8b1a3f9b4b8ba"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; schrieb am Mittwoch, 21. Oktober 2026 um 17:35:54 
&gt; UTC+2:
&gt;
&gt;&gt; *&gt; Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests 
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?*
&gt;&gt; *&gt; No*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Could a WPT be added for this?
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; On Wednesday, October 21, 2026 at 8:29:53 AM UTC-7 Chris Harrelson wrote:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; Please file a Mozilla standards request for this feature. (No need to 
&gt;&gt;&gt; file a WebKit one, since they are opposed to WebBluetooth.)
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; On Tue, Oct 20, 2026 at 11:22 AM Helmut Januschka &lt;&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="d8b0b2b9b6adf6f6f698bfb5b9b1b4f6bbb7b5"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; 
&gt;&gt;&gt; wrote:
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Contact emails*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="9cf4f6fdf2e9b2b2b2dcfbf1fdf5f0b2fff3f1"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Explainer*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebBluetoothCG/web-bluetooth/pull/672"&gt;https://github.com/WebBluetoothCG/web-bluetooth/pull/672&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Specification*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://pr-preview.s3.amazonaws.com/hjanuschka/web-bluetooth/pull/672.html#dom-bluetoothremotegattserver-maxwritewithoutresponsesize"&gt;https://pr-preview.s3.amazonaws.com/hjanuschka/web-bluetooth/pull/672.html#dom-bluetoothremotegattserver-maxwritewithoutresponsesize&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Summary*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Adds BluetoothRemoteGATTServer.maxWriteWithoutResponseSize, which 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; reports the largest payload that writeValueWithoutResponse() can send in 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; one ATT packet. A maxwritewithoutresponsesizechanged event notifies 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; applications when Chrome observes the value change, allowing applications 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; to size chunks without assuming a 20-byte limit. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; it depends on: &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5138797158334464"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5138797158334464&lt;/a&gt; and 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; will be shipped together (it has 3 LGTM's; suprisingly mailing list only 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; shows 2, but chromestatus shows 3). 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; implementation: 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/7879985"&gt;https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/7879985&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Bug: 40265040, 40686244, 40163619
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Blink component*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Blink&gt;Bluetooth 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3EBluetooth%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3EBluetooth%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; web-bluetooth &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/web-bluetooth"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/web-bluetooth&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Motivation*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Web Bluetooth applications currently cannot determine the largest 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; payload accepted by writeValueWithoutResponse() for an active GATT 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; connection. They must hard-code the 20-byte default, use platform-specific 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; heuristics, or probe with writes and handle platform-dependent failures or 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; truncation. Exposing the usable payload size allows applications to chunk 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; transfers correctly and use larger ATT MTUs for substantially better 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; throughput.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Initial public proposal*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebBluetoothCG/web-bluetooth/issues/383"&gt;https://github.com/WebBluetoothCG/web-bluetooth/issues/383&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *No information provided*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review status*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Not applicable
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Risks*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Chrome would be the first implementation of this API. Firefox does not 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; implement Web Bluetooth and Mozilla's position on Web Bluetooth is 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; negative: &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/95"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/95&lt;/a&gt;. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Safari does not implement Web Bluetooth and WebKit's position is oppose: 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/570"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/570&lt;/a&gt;. The change 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; is additive, so existing sites are unaffected and sites can feature-detect 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the attribute. The value can become available at different times on 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; different operating systems because their Bluetooth APIs expose ATT MTU 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; information differently. The specification therefore defines the value as 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the user agent's current known maximum and fires the change event when the 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; user agent observes an update.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Gecko*: No signal
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebKit*: No signal
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web developers*: No signals
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Other signals*:
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Ergonomics*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The API exposes the directly useful maximum write-without-response 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; payload instead of the raw ATT MTU, so developers do not need to subtract 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the three-byte ATT Write Command header. The synchronous property avoids 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; additional I/O and the event lets long-running transfers react when Chrome 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; learns a larger value. Applications should read the property at the point 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; of use or listen for the event because some platforms report the final 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; value later than connection establishment.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Security*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This adds a read-only numeric property and an event to an existing, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; permission-gated Web Bluetooth GATT connection. It does not grant access 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; to 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; additional devices, services, characteristics, or write operations. The 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; value can reveal a small amount of information about the selected 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; peripheral and platform Bluetooth stack after the user has granted device 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; access and established a connection. Existing Web Bluetooth 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; secure-context, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; user-consent, service allowlist, blocklist, and Permissions Policy 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; protections continue to apply. The implementation is behind 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebBluetoothMaxWriteWithoutResponseSize, which remains available as a kill 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; switch after launch.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebView application risks*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; that it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications?
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Low. This is strictly additive and behind a feature flag. Android 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebView does not expose Web Bluetooth, so the new API is not available 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; there.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Debuggability*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The value is directly inspectable in DevTools as 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; device.gatt.maxWriteWithoutResponseSize. Developers can use the Console, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; JavaScript debugger, and standard event-listener tooling for 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; maxwritewithoutresponsesizechanged. No dedicated DevTools panel or 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; protocol 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; support is required.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Mac, Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Supported on Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS, and Android wherever Web 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Bluetooth is available. Android WebView does not expose Web Bluetooth, so 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; this is not supported on all six Blink platforms.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Flag name on about://flags*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *No information provided*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Finch feature name*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebBluetoothMaxWriteWithoutResponseSize
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Rollout plan*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Will ship enabled for all users
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; False
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Tracking bug*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40265040"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/40265040&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Non-OSS dependencies*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Does the feature depend on any code or APIs outside the Chromium open 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; source repository and its open-source dependencies to function?
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None beyond the platform Bluetooth APIs already used by Chromium's Web 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Bluetooth implementation: Android BluetoothGatt, CoreBluetooth, WinRT 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; GATT, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and BlueZ D-Bus.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Estimated milestones*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on desktop 160
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on Android 160
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on WebView 160
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on iOS 160
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Anticipated spec changes*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; of 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way).
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The API shape has converged in WebBluetoothCG PR 672 and the PR is 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; approved. No further substantive changes are anticipated, but the PR must 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; be merged before shipping.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5177569273053184?gate=6176654583005184"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5177569273053184?gate=6176654583005184&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Links to previous Intent discussions*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Intent to Prototype: 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/CAFmjHKQ0ftKQn-37UikGJC2Zos%2BGyiOw7%2B5t-nx8NnRTckOUiQ%40mail.gmail.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/CAFmjHKQ0ftKQn-37UikGJC2Zos%2BGyiOw7%2B5t-nx8NnRTckOUiQ%40mail.gmail.com&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/&lt;/a&gt;&gt;.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
