&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Re%5C%3A+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+WebTransport+receive+stream%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;[blink-dev] Re: Intent to Ship: WebTransport receive stream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Alex+Russell%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Alex Russell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261027" rel="nofollow"&gt;Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:04:03 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;LGTM1; love to see these practical additions!

On Wednesday, October 21, 2026 at 6:31:37 PM UTC-7 Chromestatus wrote:&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

&gt; *Contact emails*
&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="572135252e3f3e797979173a3e342538243831237934383a"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Explainer*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/372"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/372&lt;/a&gt;
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/pull/395"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/pull/395&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Specification*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://w3c.github.io/webtransport/#webtransportreceivestream"&gt;https://w3c.github.io/webtransport/#webtransportreceivestream&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;
&gt; *Summary*
&gt; Adds the standardized WebTransportReceiveStream interface, a 
&gt; ReadableStream subclass used for incoming unidirectional streams and the 
&gt; readable side of bidirectional streams. Its getStats() method returns 
&gt; per-stream byte counters: bytesReceived reports bytes received from the 
&gt; network, while bytesRead reports bytes delivered to the application. 
&gt;
&gt; *Blink component*
&gt; Blink&gt;Network&gt;WebTransport 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Web Feature ID*
&gt; webtransport &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport&lt;/a&gt;&gt; 
&gt;
&gt; *Motivation*
&gt; Incoming WebTransport streams have historically been exposed as generic 
&gt; ReadableStream objects in Chromium. The WebTransport specification defines 
&gt; a dedicated WebTransportReceiveStream subclass with stream-specific 
&gt; statistics. Applications that receive large or latency-sensitive streams 
&gt; need to distinguish bytes received by the browser from bytes actually 
&gt; consumed by JavaScript. This helps diagnose application backpressure, 
&gt; buffering, and slow-consumer behavior. Exposing the standardized subclass 
&gt; also aligns the runtime type of incoming streams with the WebTransport 
&gt; specification and other browser engines. 
&gt;
&gt; *Initial public proposal*
&gt; *No information provided*
&gt;
&gt; *TAG review*
&gt; *No information provided* 
&gt;
&gt; *TAG review status*
&gt; Not applicable
&gt;
&gt; *Goals for experimentation*
&gt; None 
&gt;
&gt; *Risks*
&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility*
&gt; Enabling this feature changes incoming unidirectional streams and the 
&gt; readable side of bidirectional streams from plain ReadableStream  objects 
&gt; to WebTransportReceiveStream objects. Reading remains compatible, and the 
&gt; objects remain instanceof ReadableStream, but applications that compare the 
&gt; exact constructor or prototype may observe a change. Firefox and Safari 
&gt; already expose WebTransportReceiveStream, so this aligns Chrome’s stream 
&gt; type with those browsers. 
&gt;
&gt; *Gecko*: Under consideration (
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/webidl/WebTransportSendReceiveStream.webidl"&gt;https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/webidl/WebTransportSendReceiveStream.webidl&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;  Partial 
&gt; implementation. Firefox ships WebTransportReceiveStream and exposes 
&gt; getStats(), but getStats() currently rejects with NotSupportedError, so 
&gt; receive stream statistics are not implemented. Implementation 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/webtransport/api/WebTransportReceiveStream.cpp"&gt;https://searchfox.org/mozilla-central/source/dom/webtransport/api/WebTransportReceiveStream.cpp&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *WebKit*: Shipped/Shipping (&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://commits.webkit.org/300850@main"&gt;https://commits.webkit.org/300850@main&lt;/a&gt;) 
&gt; WebKit-7624.1.16 
&gt; implements WebTransportReceiveStream and pipes real receive-stream 
&gt; statistics through WebCore and the network process. WebTransport shipped in 
&gt; Safari 26.4. Implementation - 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/WebKit-7624.1.16/Source/WebCore/Modules/webtransport/WebTransportReceiveStream.cpp#L54-L65"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/WebKit-7624.1.16/Source/WebCore/Modules/webtransport/WebTransportReceiveStream.cpp#L54-L65&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;  
&gt;
&gt; *Web developers*: Positive (
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121"&gt;https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121&lt;/a&gt;) The Interop 
&gt; 2026 WebTransport proposal received 28 positive reactions. Developers also 
&gt; reported active WebTransport use and significant cross-engine differences.
&gt;
&gt; *Other signals*:
&gt;
&gt; *Ergonomics*
&gt; getStats() asynchronously returns a snapshot with bytesReceived and 
&gt; bytesRead. bytesRead is expected to be less than or equal to bytesReceived. 
&gt; Chromium preserves final counters when streams finish, are reset or 
&gt; cancelled, or when the Mojo connection disconnects. The interface is not 
&gt; constructible directly; applications obtain it from 
&gt; incomingUnidirectionalStreams or from the readable side of a bidirectional 
&gt; stream. Chromium's IDL currently types 
&gt; WebTransportBidirectionalStream.readable as ReadableStream because Blink 
&gt; cannot conditionally change an IDL attribute type behind a runtime flag, 
&gt; although the returned object is a WebTransportReceiveStream when the 
&gt; feature is enabled.
&gt;
&gt; *Activation*
&gt; Activation is automatic for sites using incoming WebTransport streams; no 
&gt; server change or opt-in is required. Existing code that treats these 
&gt; objects as ReadableStream continues to work because 
&gt; WebTransportReceiveStream is a subclass. The runtime type and prototype are 
&gt; observably different, so code that relies on an exact ReadableStream 
&gt; constructor or prototype could behave differently. Feature detection using 
&gt; &quot;getStats&quot; in stream is recommended.
&gt;
&gt; *Security*
&gt; The API adds no network capability and exposes no peer address, 
&gt; credentials, or payload contents. It reports byte counters for a stream 
&gt; already available to the calling origin. These values reveal only the 
&gt; amount of data received by the browser and consumed by that application.
&gt;
&gt; *WebView application risks*
&gt;
&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such that 
&gt; it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? 
&gt; Low. This is an additive API on existing WebTransport streams and does not 
&gt; change connection establishment or network behavior. WebView code that does 
&gt; not inspect the stream's exact runtime type is unaffected. 
&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Debuggability*
&gt; Developers can inspect incoming streams and call getStats() from the 
&gt; DevTools Console. The returned bytesReceived and bytesRead values help 
&gt; distinguish network delivery from application consumption. DevTools does 
&gt; not currently provide a dedicated per-WebTransport-stream statistics UI. 
&gt;
&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, Mac, 
&gt; Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?*
&gt; Yes
&gt;
&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?*
&gt; Yes 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/receive-stream-stats.https.any.html"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/receive-stream-stats.https.any.html&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Flag name on about://flags*
&gt; enable-experimental-web-platform-features 
&gt;
&gt; *Finch feature name*
&gt; WebTransportReceiveStream 
&gt;
&gt; *Rollout plan*
&gt; Will ship enabled for all users
&gt;
&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?*
&gt; False
&gt;
&gt; *Tracking bug*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/568317222"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/568317222&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Availability expectation*
&gt; The interface is defined in the W3C WebTransport specification. WebKit 
&gt; implements WebTransportReceiveStream and real receive-stream statistics. 
&gt; Firefox exposes WebTransportReceiveStream, but its getStats() 
&gt; implementation currently rejects with NotSupportedError. Implementations 
&gt; are expected to converge through the Interop 2026 WebTransport focus area.
&gt;
&gt; *Adoption expectation*
&gt; Expected to be adopted by WebTransport applications that monitor per 
&gt; stream throughput, buffering, or slow-consumer behavior. Code that only 
&gt; reads from incoming streams does not need to change.
&gt;
&gt; *Adoption plan*
&gt; Adoption is expected to occur organically through WebTransport 
&gt; documentation, examples, and client library support. Developers can 
&gt; progressively enhance existing code by feature-detecting getStats().
&gt;
&gt; *Non-OSS dependencies*
&gt;
&gt; Does the feature depend on any code or APIs outside the Chromium open 
&gt; source repository and its open-source dependencies to function? 
&gt; No
&gt;
&gt; *Estimated milestones*
&gt; Shipping on desktop 158 
&gt; DevTrial on desktop 155 
&gt; Shipping on Android 158 
&gt; DevTrial on Android 155 
&gt; Shipping on WebView 158 
&gt;
&gt; *Anticipated spec changes*
&gt;
&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or 
&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues 
&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may 
&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure of 
&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). 
&gt; None known for the WebTransportReceiveStream interface or the 
&gt; bytesReceived and bytesRead members.
&gt;
&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5109912169480192?gate=6063689550659584"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5109912169480192?gate=6063689550659584&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com"&gt;https://chromestatus.com&lt;/a&gt;&gt;. 
&gt;

-- 
