&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Re%5C%3A+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+WebTransport+Datagram+writable+streams+and+prioritization%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;[blink-dev] Re: Intent to Ship: WebTransport Datagram writable streams and prioritization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Alex+Russell%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Alex Russell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261027" rel="nofollow"&gt;Tue, 27 Oct 2026 12:07:17 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;LGTM1; we've had intermittent feedback over the years about how 
WebTransport wasn't well suited to latency-sensitive work, and it's great 
to see these additions.&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

On Thursday, October 22, 2026 at 3:11:29 PM UTC-7 Monica Chintala wrote:

&gt; FYI, this feature depends on WebTransport send groups and stream 
&gt; prioritization - Chrome Platform Status 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://nam06.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fchromestatus.com%2Ffeature%2F5235261159112704&amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmonicach%40microsoft.com%7C9e0b9f4149a541fafafe08df307d863d%7C72f988bf86f141af91ab2d7cd011db47%7C1%7C0%7C639282987879917270%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;sdata=fMplyzw4jssGTpxdGq0wgG5zQ2s6HhhTQI6b1Zm6cps%3D&amp;reserved=0"&gt;https://nam06.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fchromestatus.com%2Ffeature%2F5235261159112704&amp;data=05%7C02%7Cmonicach%40microsoft.com%7C9e0b9f4149a541fafafe08df307d863d%7C72f988bf86f141af91ab2d7cd011db47%7C1%7C0%7C639282987879917270%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp;sdata=fMplyzw4jssGTpxdGq0wgG5zQ2s6HhhTQI6b1Zm6cps%3D&amp;reserved=0&lt;/a&gt;&gt;,
&gt;  
&gt; which is currently in progress for I2S. I'm working on getting the 
&gt; necessary approvals now so we're ready to proceed, while ensuring this 
&gt; feature ships only after the dependent feature has shipped.
&gt;
&gt; On Thursday, October 22, 2026 at 2:24:42 PM UTC-7 Chromestatus wrote:
&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Contact emails*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="e09289cececea08388928f8d89958dce8f9287"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="a1cccecfc88f8f8fe1ccc8c2d3ced2cec7d58fc2cecc"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Explainer*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#5-sending-and-receiving-datagrams"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#5-sending-and-receiving-datagrams&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Specification*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#datagram-writable"&gt;https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#datagram-writable&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Summary*
&gt;&gt; Adds the modern WebTransport Datagram sending API. 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportDatagramDuplexStream.createWritable() creates independent 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportDatagramsWritable streams. Each writable has mutable sendGroup 
&gt;&gt; and sendOrder attributes for expressing relative priority among queued 
&gt;&gt; WebTransport data in the same send group. Chromium currently applies this 
&gt;&gt; ordering among Datagram writables. The implementation also reports the 
&gt;&gt; negotiated maximum outgoing Datagram payload size and discards oversized 
&gt;&gt; writes as required by the WebTransport specification. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Blink component*
&gt;&gt; Blink&gt;Network&gt;WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID*
&gt;&gt; webtransport &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport&lt;/a&gt;&gt; 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Motivation*
&gt;&gt; The original WebTransport Datagram API exposes only a single writable 
&gt;&gt; stream, preventing applications from independently scheduling different 
&gt;&gt; classes of latency-sensitive messages. The newer API allows applications 
&gt;&gt; such as games, remote-interaction systems, and real-time media applications 
&gt;&gt; to create multiple Datagram writables and prioritize urgent data over less 
&gt;&gt; time-sensitive data while preserving FIFO ordering within each writable. 
&gt;&gt; Implementing this API also resolves WebTransport Datagram interoperability 
&gt;&gt; failures included in Interop 2026. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Initial public proposal*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/451"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/451&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *TAG review*
&gt;&gt; This is an incremental API addition within the existing WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; specification and does not introduce a new architectural primitive. A 
&gt;&gt; separate feature-level TAG review is not planned. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *TAG review status*
&gt;&gt; Not applicable
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation*
&gt;&gt; None 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Risks*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility*
&gt;&gt; Interoperability risk Medium. The API is defined in the WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; Candidate Recommendation and covered by WPT. Implementations may 
&gt;&gt; nevertheless differ in scheduling boundaries, particularly priority 
&gt;&gt; interaction between streams and Datagrams. Chromium currently implements 
&gt;&gt; ordering among Datagram writables; unified stream-and-Datagram arbitration 
&gt;&gt; may require additional implementation work and specification clarification. 
&gt;&gt; Compatibility risk Low. The API adds createWritable() and 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportDatagramsWritable without removing datagrams.writable. Existing 
&gt;&gt; applications continue to work. The negotiated maxDatagramSize and 
&gt;&gt; oversized-write behavior are intentional conformance improvements but could 
&gt;&gt; expose applications that assumed a fixed outgoing Datagram limit. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Gecko*: Shipped/Shipping (
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2007174"&gt;https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2007174&lt;/a&gt;) Firefox 
&gt;&gt; implemented and related wpt tests are passing
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *WebKit*: Shipped/Shipping (&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/53733"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/53733&lt;/a&gt;) 
&gt;&gt; WebKit 
&gt;&gt; implemented and related wpt tests are passing 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Web developers*: Positive (
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121"&gt;https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121&lt;/a&gt;) The Interop 
&gt;&gt; 2026 WebTransport proposal received 28 👍 reactions and explicitly 
&gt;&gt; requested coverage of prioritization features and Datagram 
&gt;&gt; interoperability. Developers also reported active WebTransport use and 
&gt;&gt; significant cross-browser differences in Datagram behavior.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Other signals*: Consensus to add this feature was achieved at the W3C 
&gt;&gt; TPAC conference in November 2025: 
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.w3.org/wiki/WebTransport/Meetings2023#2025_W3C_TPAC_Meeting_-_Kobe,_Japan"&gt;https://www.w3.org/wiki/WebTransport/Meetings2023#2025_W3C_TPAC_Meeting_-_Kobe,_Japan&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Ergonomics*
&gt;&gt; Low to medium. Multiple writable streams and mutable sendGroup and 
&gt;&gt; sendOrder attributes provide useful scheduling control but introduce 
&gt;&gt; concepts developers must understand. Higher sendOrder values take 
&gt;&gt; precedence within a send group, so badly chosen priorities can starve 
&gt;&gt; lower-priority data or produce unexpected ordering. FIFO behavior within 
&gt;&gt; each writable and standardized defaults preserve straightforward behavior 
&gt;&gt; for applications that do not need advanced prioritization.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Activation*
&gt;&gt; Low. This is an additive API behind the WebTransportDatagramsWritable 
&gt;&gt; runtime feature. It does not remove or replace the existing 
&gt;&gt; datagrams.writable API. Enabling the feature also makes maxDatagramSize 
&gt;&gt; reflect the negotiated outgoing payload limit and discards writes that 
&gt;&gt; exceed that limit, as required by the specification. The main activation 
&gt;&gt; risk is inconsistent priority scheduling across browsers, particularly 
&gt;&gt; around the boundary between streams and Datagrams. Rollout should follow 
&gt;&gt; WPT and interoperability validation. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Security*
&gt;&gt; Low. The API does not grant new network access, expose additional data, 
&gt;&gt; or weaken WebTransport's secure-context requirements. The primary 
&gt;&gt; implementation concern is renderer-driven resource exhaustion through 
&gt;&gt; excessive writable registrations or queued Datagram payloads. Chromium 
&gt;&gt; mitigates this through network-service validation and limits on writable 
&gt;&gt; registrations, queued Datagram counts, retained bytes, expiration, and 
&gt;&gt; scheduling batch sizes. Invalid renderer-provided identifiers and priority 
&gt;&gt; updates are rejected.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *WebView application risks*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such that 
&gt;&gt; it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? 
&gt;&gt; Low. This is an additive WebTransport API implemented in cross-platform 
&gt;&gt; Blink and network-service code. It does not remove or change the 
&gt;&gt; availability of the existing datagrams.writable API and requires no 
&gt;&gt; WebView-specific integration. Behavior follows the existing availability of 
&gt;&gt; WebTransport in WebView. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Debuggability*
&gt;&gt; Chrome DevTools does not currently provide feature-specific inspection 
&gt;&gt; for Datagram writable queues or priorities. Existing WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; diagnostics, NetLog, network-service logging, and QUIC logging can be used 
&gt;&gt; to diagnose connection and scheduling behavior 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, Mac, 
&gt;&gt; Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?*
&gt;&gt; Yes
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests 
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?*
&gt;&gt; Yes 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/idlharness.https.sub.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/idlharness.https.sub.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;  
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/datagrams.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/datagrams.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;  
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/datagrams-writable.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/datagrams-writable.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;  
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendgroup.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendgroup.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Flag name on about://flags*
&gt;&gt; enable-experimental-web-platform-features 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Finch feature name*
&gt;&gt; WebTransportDatagramsWritable 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Rollout plan*
&gt;&gt; Will ship enabled for all users
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?*
&gt;&gt; False
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Tracking bug*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/547572475"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/547572475&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Availability expectation*
&gt;&gt; The feature is expected to be available across major browsers as part of 
&gt;&gt; the W3C WebTransport specification. Firefox implements the Datagram 
&gt;&gt; writable and prioritization APIs, and WebKit has landed an implementation 
&gt;&gt; that is currently in development.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Adoption expectation*
&gt;&gt; Adoption is expected primarily among latency-sensitive WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; applications, including games, remote-interaction systems, and real-time 
&gt;&gt; media. Applications that need multiple independently prioritized Datagram 
&gt;&gt; queues are expected to begin adopting the API within 12 months of broad 
&gt;&gt; browser availability.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Adoption plan*
&gt;&gt; Adoption is expected to happen organically through WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; documentation, examples, and Interop 2026 visibility. Existing applications 
&gt;&gt; do not need to migrate because the existing datagrams.writable API remains 
&gt;&gt; available.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Estimated milestones*
&gt;&gt; Shipping on desktop 159 
&gt;&gt; DevTrial on desktop 155 
&gt;&gt; Shipping on Android 159 
&gt;&gt; DevTrial on Android 155 
&gt;&gt; Shipping on WebView 159 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Anticipated spec changes*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or 
&gt;&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues 
&gt;&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may 
&gt;&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure of 
&gt;&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). 
&gt;&gt; No API-shape changes are currently anticipated. The WebTransport Working 
&gt;&gt; Group may further clarify scheduling boundaries and priority arbitration 
&gt;&gt; between streams and Datagrams in the same send group. Such clarification is 
&gt;&gt; expected to affect scheduling behavior only, not the exposed API.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5183354944225280?gate=6105683064913920"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5183354944225280?gate=6105683064913920&lt;/a&gt;
