&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt; &lt;h1&gt; &lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Re%5C%3A+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+WebTransport+keying+material+export%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Re: Intent to Ship: WebTransport keying material export&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/h1&gt; &lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt; &lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Chris+Harrelson%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Chris Harrelson&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261027" rel="nofollow"&gt;Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:19:26 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;LGTM3
On Tue, Oct 27, 2026 at 11:37 PM Mike Taylor &lt;&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="e08d898b859481cececea08388928f8d89958dce8f9287"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;
&gt; LGTM2 &gt; On 10/27/26 7:00 p.m., Alex Russell wrote: &gt; &gt; LGTM1 &gt; &gt; On Wednesday, October 21, 2026 at 12:09:28 PM UTC-7 Chromestatus wrote: &gt; &gt;&gt; *Contact emails* &gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="5123387f7f7f113239233e3c38243c7f3e2336"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="cda0a2a3a4aee3e3e38da0a4aebfa2bea2abb9e3aea2a0"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Explainer* &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Specification* &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#dom-webtransport-exportkeyingmaterial"&gt;https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#dom-webtransport-exportkeyingmaterial&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Summary* &gt;&gt; Adds WebTransport.exportKeyingMaterial(), which allows an application to &gt;&gt; derive cryptographic keying material bound to an established WebTransport &gt;&gt; session. The method accepts application provided binary label and context &gt;&gt; values and a requested output length and returns a Promise&lt;Uint8Array&gt;. &gt;&gt; Chromium supports label and context values up to 255 bytes and output &gt;&gt; lengths from 1 through 4096 bytes. For WebTransport over HTTP/3, Chromium &gt;&gt; includes the WebTransport CONNECT stream ID in the TLS exporter context. &gt;&gt; This ensures that separate WebTransport sessions derive different keying &gt;&gt; material even when they share the same underlying HTTP/3 connection. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Blink component* &gt;&gt; Blink&gt;Network&gt;WebTransport &gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID* &gt;&gt; webtransport &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Motivation* &gt;&gt; Applications sometimes need cryptographic keying material bound to an &gt;&gt; authenticated transport session, for example to bind an application &gt;&gt; protocol, authentication exchange, or application-level encryption context &gt;&gt; to a WebTransport session without performing an additional key exchange. &gt;&gt; TLS exporters derive application-specific secret material without exposing &gt;&gt; the TLS traffic keys. WebTransport.exportKeyingMaterial() exposes this &gt;&gt; mechanism through application-provided binary label and context values and &gt;&gt; an explicit output length. Multiple WebTransport sessions can share one &gt;&gt; HTTP/3 connection. Chromium therefore includes the WebTransport CONNECT &gt;&gt; stream ID in the exporter context, ensuring that different sessions derive &gt;&gt; different keying material even when callers use identical application &gt;&gt; labels and contexts. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Initial public proposal* &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/411"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/411&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *TAG review* &gt;&gt; This is an incremental method within the existing WebTransport &gt;&gt; specification. WebTransport has an umbrella TAG review, and a separate &gt;&gt; feature-level review is not currently planned. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *TAG review status* &gt;&gt; Not applicable &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation* &gt;&gt; None &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Risks* &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility* &gt;&gt; Interoperability risk: Medium. The current WebTransport specification and &gt;&gt; upstream WPT define the required three-argument &gt;&gt; exportKeyingMaterial(label, context, outputLength) method. Chromium and &gt;&gt; WebKit implement this shape. Firefox currently exposes the earlier &gt;&gt; two-argument form without outputLength , so API-shape interoperability is &gt;&gt; not yet complete. Implementations and servers must also use the same fixed &gt;&gt; exporter label and exporter-context construction, including the &gt;&gt; WebTransport session ID, application label, application context, and &gt;&gt; requested output length. A mismatch produces different bytes without an &gt;&gt; explicit protocol error. Compatibility risk: Low. The method is additive &gt;&gt; and does not change existing WebTransport construction, streams, datagrams, &gt;&gt; or connection behavior. Applications can use feature detection with &gt;&gt; &quot;exportKeyingMaterial&quot; in WebTransport.prototype. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Gecko*: Positive (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2079306"&gt;https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2079306&lt;/a&gt;) &gt;&gt; Firefox &gt;&gt; is updating its implementation to the current three-argument API. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *WebKit*: In development (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/69982"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/pull/69982&lt;/a&gt;) WebKit &gt;&gt; has landed an implementation of the current three-argument &gt;&gt; WebTransport.exportKeyingMaterial(label, context, outputLength) API. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Web developers*: Positive ( &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/411"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/411&lt;/a&gt;) The proposal received 10 &gt;&gt; 👍 reactions and requests from developers, including libp2p, for &gt;&gt; TLS-session binding and channel-binding use cases. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Other signals*: &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Ergonomics* &gt;&gt; Ergonomics risk is moderate because this is a low-level cryptographic &gt;&gt; primitive. Binary BufferSource label and context values avoid &gt;&gt; string-encoding ambiguity, while the explicit output length avoids an &gt;&gt; implicit key-size choice. Calls made while the WebTransport session is &gt;&gt; connecting are queued until the connection is established. Calls after &gt;&gt; closure or failure reject with InvalidStateError . Label or context values &gt;&gt; over 255 bytes and zero or unsupported output lengths reject with &gt;&gt; RangeError . Documentation should recommend protocol-specific &gt;&gt; domain-separation values and secure handling of the resulting bytes. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Activation* &gt;&gt; Activation risk is moderate but bounded. The API is additive and has no &gt;&gt; effect unless a site explicitly invokes it. It introduces no new network &gt;&gt; messages or additional protocol negotiation. The main activation risk is a &gt;&gt; mismatch between client and server implementations in the exporter &gt;&gt; construction, including the fixed exporter label, WebTransport session ID &gt;&gt; serialization, application label or context, and output length. Such a &gt;&gt; mismatch causes the endpoints to derive different bytes without an explicit &gt;&gt; error. Chromium mitigates this risk through validation in Blink and across &gt;&gt; the renderer/network-service boundary, fixed input limits, session-specific &gt;&gt; domain separation, lifecycle checks, unit and Web Platform Tests, and the &gt;&gt; WebTransportExportKeyingMaterial runtime/Finch feature gate. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Security* &gt;&gt; This API exposes secret-derived material to script and therefore requires &gt;&gt; careful domain separation and lifecycle handling. It does not expose TLS &gt;&gt; traffic keys or allow callers to decrypt WebTransport traffic. Chromium &gt;&gt; uses the fixed TLS exporter label &quot;EXPORTER-WebTransport&quot; and constructs &gt;&gt; an exporter context containing the WebTransport CONNECT stream ID, &gt;&gt; application label, and application context. Including the CONNECT stream ID &gt;&gt; prevents different WebTransport sessions sharing one HTTP/3 connection from &gt;&gt; deriving identical keying material when given the same application inputs. &gt;&gt; Chromium limits label and context values to 255 bytes and output to 4096 &gt;&gt; bytes, validates requests in both Blink and the network service, verifies &gt;&gt; the returned output length, and rejects calls after the session has closed &gt;&gt; or failed. Applications remain responsible for treating the result as &gt;&gt; secret keying material and using protocol-specific label and context values &gt;&gt; Security questionnaire: &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/security-questionnaire.md"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/security-questionnaire.md&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *WebView application risks* &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such that &gt;&gt; it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? &gt;&gt; Low. This is an additive API implemented in shared Blink and &gt;&gt; network-service code. The CL updates the Android WebView Web-exposed &gt;&gt; interface expectation and requires no WebView-specific integration. Stable &gt;&gt; WebView exposure should follow the existing availability and launch process &gt;&gt; for WebTransport in WebView. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Debuggability* &gt;&gt; Developers can inspect the label, context, output length, returned &gt;&gt; Uint8Array, and any rejected exception through JavaScript breakpoints or &gt;&gt; the Console. CDP exposes WebTransport creation, connection-establishment, &gt;&gt; and closure events. NetLog provides additional WebTransport version, QUIC &gt;&gt; lifecycle, and stream diagnostics. Neither CDP nor NetLog records exporter &gt;&gt; invocations, exported bytes, or compares the client-derived value with the &gt;&gt; server-derived value. A client/server derivation mismatch therefore &gt;&gt; requires server cooperation, preferably through a key-confirmation &gt;&gt; challenge or MAC rather than logging raw keying material. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, Mac, &gt;&gt; Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?* &gt;&gt; Yes &gt;&gt; All Blink platforms on which WebTransport over HTTP/3 is supported. The &gt;&gt; Web-exposed implementation is cross-platform Blink code, while key &gt;&gt; derivation uses Chromium’s existing HTTP/3/QUIC TLS exporter path. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests &gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?* &gt;&gt; Yes &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/export-keying-material.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/export-keying-material.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt;, &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/idlharness.https.sub.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/idlharness.https.sub.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Flag name on about://flags* &gt;&gt; chrome://flags/#enable-experimental-web-platform-features &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Finch feature name* &gt;&gt; WebTransportExportKeyingMaterial &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Rollout plan* &gt;&gt; Will ship enabled for all users &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?* &gt;&gt; False &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Tracking bug* &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/556304550"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/556304550&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Measurement* &gt;&gt; Measure correctness and interoperability through Web Platform Tests, the &gt;&gt; WebTransport IDL harness, wpt.fyi results, and feedback from WebTransport &gt;&gt; application and server implementers. Usage will be measured with a &gt;&gt; WebFeature use counter recorded when WebTransport.exportKeyingMaterial() &gt;&gt; is called. No dedicated UMA metric is currently planned, the use counter is &gt;&gt; sufficient to measure web-exposed API adoption &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Availability expectation* &gt;&gt; Expected to become available across major browser engines as part of the &gt;&gt; W3C WebTransport specification. WebKit has landed the current &gt;&gt; three-argument API in WebKit main, although stable Safari availability has &gt;&gt; not yet been verified. Firefox currently implements the earlier &gt;&gt; two-argument form without the required outputLength argument. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Adoption expectation* &gt;&gt; Expected to be used by specialized WebTransport protocols that require &gt;&gt; transport-bound authentication, channel binding, or application key &gt;&gt; derivation. It is not expected to be used by most basic WebTransport &gt;&gt; applications. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Adoption plan* &gt;&gt; Adoption is expected to occur through WebTransport documentation, &gt;&gt; interoperable WPT coverage, and use by protocol implementations that &gt;&gt; require transport-bound keying material. No origin trial or broad developer &gt;&gt; campaign is currently planned &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Estimated milestones* &gt;&gt; Shipping on desktop 158 &gt;&gt; Shipping on Android 158 &gt;&gt; Shipping on WebView 158 &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Anticipated spec changes* &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or &gt;&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues &gt;&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may &gt;&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure of &gt;&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). &gt;&gt; No API-shape changes are currently anticipated. Chromium implements the &gt;&gt; current required three-argument method and matches the &gt;&gt; WebTransport-over-HTTP/3 exporter construction, including the fixed &gt;&gt; exporter label and session-specific context encoding. &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/791"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/issues/791&lt;/a&gt; tracks making the &gt;&gt; specification’s reference to the protocol-level exporter construction more &gt;&gt; explicit. This is currently an editorial clarity issue rather than a known &gt;&gt; behavior change. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status* &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/4860330806214656?gate=6662931365363712"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/4860330806214656?gate=6662931365363712&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; *Links to previous Intent discussions* &gt;&gt; Intent to Prototype: &gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6aa97628.f13237a8.13ec.001a.GAE%40google.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6aa97628.f13237a8.13ec.001a.GAE%40google.com&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status &gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com"&gt;https://chromestatus.com&lt;/a&gt;&gt;. &gt;&gt; &gt; -- &gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &gt; &quot;blink-dev&quot; group. &gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an &gt; email to &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="95f7f9fcfbfeb8f1f0e3bee0fbe6e0f7e6f6e7bbbbbbd5f6fde7faf8fce0f8bbfae7f2"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;. &gt; To view this discussion visit &gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/3a5388d9-892a-48a3-b39a-ebc57635aa98n%40chromium.org"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/3a5388d9-892a-48a3-b39a-ebc57635aa98n%40chromium.org&lt;/a&gt; &gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/3a5388d9-892a-48a3-b39a-ebc57635aa98n%40chromium.org?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/3a5388d9-892a-48a3-b39a-ebc57635aa98n%40chromium.org?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt; . &gt; &gt; -- &gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &gt; &quot;blink-dev&quot; group. &gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an &gt; email to &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="5b3937323530763f3e2d702e35282e392838297575751b3833293436322e367534293c"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;. &gt; To view this discussion visit &gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/96b5ba8b-fc57-4a51-96d2-1a75b4d1de5d%40chromium.org"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/96b5ba8b-fc57-4a51-96d2-1a75b4d1de5d%40chromium.org&lt;/a&gt; &gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/96b5ba8b-fc57-4a51-96d2-1a75b4d1de5d%40chromium.org?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/96b5ba8b-fc57-4a51-96d2-1a75b4d1de5d%40chromium.org?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt; . &gt;
-- You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;blink-dev&quot; group. To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="3c5e505552571158594a1749524f495e4f5f4e1212127c5f544e535155495112534e5b"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;. To view this discussion visit &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/CAOMQ%2Bw9j0MTrEkwaXS0OAOn49i7uoD5MRUniEN%2B1VH58JbWpTQ%40mail.gmail.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/CAOMQ%2Bw9j0MTrEkwaXS0OAOn49i7uoD5MRUniEN%2B1VH58JbWpTQ%40mail.gmail.com&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;/pre&gt;
