/Other signals/:
*Ergonomics* This change makes `AccentColor` and `AccentColorText` behave consistently with accent-color styling defined by the specification. It does not introduce new APIs or syntax and is expected to reduce developer confusion by making these keywords reflect the colors authors already use for `accent-color`.
*Activation* No developer action is required to enable or adopt this feature. Existing use of `AccentColor` and `AccentColorText` automatically receives the updated spec-defined behavior.
*Security* This feature does not expose new information, permissions, or browser capabilities. It only changes how existing CSS color keywords resolve in accordance with the CSS Color Module Level 4 specification.
*WebView application risks* /Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such that it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications?/ None, any change is cosmetic.
*Debuggability* The resulting colors are visible through existing CSS inspection and debugging tools. Developers can inspect `accent-color`, `AccentColor`, and `AccentColorText` using standard DevTools workflows without requiring new debugging surfaces.
*Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, Mac, Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?* Yes
