&gt;
&gt;&gt; Thank you Mike for the LGTM!
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Philip, following up on my earlier reply, I'd like to adjust the plan a 
&gt;&gt; little bit. The policy would still apply to autofocus, but I'd keep 
&gt;&gt; Chromium's existing cross-origin restriction in place for now. Authors 
&gt;&gt; could use the policy to further restrict otherwise permitted autofocus, but 
&gt;&gt; allowing it wouldn't override that restriction.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; One of my main arguments was that a frame with the policy allowed could 
&gt;&gt; just call focus() anyway, but that argument misses scriptless cross-origin 
&gt;&gt; content, like a document with script-src 'none', or sanitized HTML that 
&gt;&gt; still includes autofocus.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; I filed WHATWG #13105 &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/13105"&gt;https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/13105&lt;/a&gt;&gt; to 
&gt;&gt; revisit whether this policy should lift the cross-origin autofocus 
&gt;&gt; restriction, and how the spec and tests should line up.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Under this plan, the autofocus assertion would still fail against the 
&gt;&gt; current spec for the initial rollout. Would you be comfortable moving ahead 
&gt;&gt; with the rest of the policy while we work through the autofocus question 
&gt;&gt; separately?
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Thanks,
&gt;&gt; Fernando
