&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+Permissions+Policy%5C%3A+focus%5C-without%5C-user%5C-activation%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Intent to Ship: Permissions Policy: focus-without-user-activation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22%27Fernando+Fiori%27+via+blink%5C-dev%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;'Fernando Fiori' via blink-dev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261020" rel="nofollow"&gt;Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:19:06 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;

Thank you Mike for the LGTM!&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;


Philip, following up on my earlier reply, I'd like to adjust the plan a 
little bit. The policy would still apply to autofocus, but I'd keep 
Chromium's existing cross-origin restriction in place for now. Authors 
could use the policy to further restrict otherwise permitted autofocus, but 
allowing it wouldn't override that restriction.

One of my main arguments was that a frame with the policy allowed could 
just call focus() anyway, but that argument misses scriptless cross-origin 
content, like a document with script-src 'none', or sanitized HTML that 
still includes autofocus.

I filed WHATWG #13105 &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/13105"&gt;https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/13105&lt;/a&gt;&gt; to 
revisit whether this policy should lift the cross-origin autofocus 
restriction, and how the spec and tests should line up.

Under this plan, the autofocus assertion would still fail against the 
current spec for the initial rollout. Would you be comfortable moving ahead 
with the rest of the policy while we work through the autofocus question 
separately?


Thanks,
Fernando
On Monday, October 19, 2026 at 6:09:17 PM UTC-7 &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="8fe2e6e4eaa1a1a1cfece7fde0e2e6fae2a1e0fde8"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; wrote:

&gt; This looks like a good feature. LGTM2
&gt; On 10/19/26 7:54 p.m., 'Fernando Fiori' via blink-dev wrote:
&gt;
&gt; Thank you, Alex, for the LGTM! 
&gt;
&gt; Thomas, thanks for having a look. Those points you raise are precisely 
&gt; what this permissions policy aims to address. I tried to capture this in 
&gt; more detail in the explainer 
&gt; webappsec-permissions-policy/policies/focus-without-user-activation.md 
&gt; at ffiori/webappsec-permissions-policy 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ffiori/webappsec-permissions-policy/blob/focus-without-user-activation-explainer/policies/focus-without-user-activation.md"&gt;https://github.com/ffiori/webappsec-permissions-policy/blob/focus-without-user-activation-explainer/policies/focus-without-user-activation.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;.
&gt;  
&gt; Let me know if you have any questions.
&gt;
&gt; Fernando
&gt;
&gt; On Monday, October 19, 2026 at 4:19:14 PM UTC-7 &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="64100c0b09051707080d4a4a4a240309050d084a070b09"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt; wrote:
&gt;
&gt;&gt; not clear how this interfaces with w3c, but here are a few threats I 
&gt;&gt; found partly from AI.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    - *Keystroke Redirection &amp; Phishing (Focus Theft):* Embedded 
&gt;&gt;    third-party content can silently pull focus away from the main page input 
&gt;&gt;    fields. If a user is typing sensitive information (e.g., credentials, 
&gt;&gt;    credit card numbers, or search queries), their keystrokes can be 
&gt;&gt; redirected 
&gt;&gt;    into an embedded iframe or form without visual or immediate notice. 
&gt;&gt;    1 &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5179186249465856"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5179186249465856&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    2 
&gt;&gt;    
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/#all/%23thread-f:1879048351712254581%7Cmsg-f:1879503045256153924"&gt;https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/#all/%23thread-f:1879048351712254581%7Cmsg-f:1879503045256153924&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    - *Accessibility Disruption for Assistive Technologies:* Screen 
&gt;&gt;    readers and keyboard-only navigation depend heavily on stable focus. 
&gt;&gt; Unprompted 
&gt;&gt;    focus changes forcibly relocate the reading or keyboard focus, confusing 
&gt;&gt;    visually or mobility-impaired users and preventing them from navigating 
&gt;&gt;    predictably. 
&gt;&gt;    3 
&gt;&gt;    
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.webaccessibility.com/resource-library/best-practice/?bestPractice=801"&gt;https://www.webaccessibility.com/resource-library/best-practice/?bestPractice=801&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    - *Keyboard Navigation Hijacking:* Users attempting to navigate forms 
&gt;&gt;    or pages via keyboard shortcuts or Tab keys can be trapped or thrown 
&gt;&gt;    off course if a script continually pulls focus back to a specific element
&gt;&gt;    . 
&gt;&gt;    3 
&gt;&gt;    
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.webaccessibility.com/resource-library/best-practice/?bestPractice=801"&gt;https://www.webaccessibility.com/resource-library/best-practice/?bestPractice=801&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    - *UI Flickering &amp; Performance Degradation:* When multiple scripts or 
&gt;&gt;    embedded frames aggressively run scripts (e.g., using element.focus(), 
&gt;&gt;    window.focus(), or blur event polling) to reclaim or maintain focus, 
&gt;&gt;    it results in visible screen flickering and erratic user interface 
&gt;&gt; behavior
&gt;&gt;    . 
&gt;&gt;    1 &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5179186249465856"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5179186249465856&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    2 
&gt;&gt;    
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/#all/%23thread-f:1879048351712254581%7Cmsg-f:1879503045256153924"&gt;https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/#all/%23thread-f:1879048351712254581%7Cmsg-f:1879503045256153924&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    - *Unwanted Modals &amp; Dialog Interruptions:* Scripts can force focus 
&gt;&gt;    into programmatic popups or dialogs (dialog.showModal(), popover 
&gt;&gt;    focusing, or autofocus), disrupting the workflow without any 
&gt;&gt;    preceding user action or gesture. 
&gt;&gt;    1 &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5179186249465856"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5179186249465856&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    
&gt;&gt; Also note that focus may become another threat with folding screens which 
&gt;&gt; now have an open possible with focus that could be major confusion.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Peace ..tom jones
&gt;&gt;
