&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt; &lt;h1&gt; &lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+Spell+check+custom+dictionary+API%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Intent to Ship: Spell check custom dictionary API&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/h1&gt; &lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt; &lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Brian+Kardell%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Brian Kardell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261029" rel="nofollow"&gt;Thu, 29 Oct 2026 04:44:59 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;Hey sorry, we've been out on hoildays last week, I am out at TPAC this week and next week at other meetings. I pushed a few changes to the PR before I left, but I need to come back to it when (I imagine many of us) are less swamped with TPAC. Happy to hear any HTML spec feedback if you've got any in the meantime.&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;
On Tuesday, October 27, 2026 at 3:52:25 PM UTC Alex Russell wrote:
&gt; Any updates here? This feature looks great and I'd *like* to give it a &gt; go-ahead if we think the spec is in better shape. &gt; &gt; On Thursday, October 15, 2026 at 7:05:34 AM UTC-7 Ziran Sun wrote: &gt; &gt;&gt; Thanks for the comment! We will look at this. &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt; On Wednesday, October 14, 2026 at 4:01:33 PM UTC+1 Philip Jägenstedt &gt;&gt; wrote: &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; Hi, &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; I see that &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12590"&gt;https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12590&lt;/a&gt; is a draft with &gt;&gt;&gt; seemingly no review of the actual spec text. If &gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646&lt;/a&gt; is going to be &gt;&gt;&gt; resolved as positive (it's not yet) then maybe you can entice a WebKit &gt;&gt;&gt; contributor to carefully review the spec? &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; In other words, I'd like to see some more effort to progress the spec &gt;&gt;&gt; here. &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; Best regards, &gt;&gt;&gt; Philip &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; On Wed, Oct 14, 2026 at 12:09 PM Chromestatus &lt; &gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="7617125858583615045b05021702030558170606050619021b171f1a5815191b"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote: &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Contact emails* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="0e747d2020204e67696f62676f206d6163"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="5320303b367d7d7d13303b213c3e3a263e7d3c2134"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Explainer* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/tree/main/spell-check-dictionary"&gt;https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/tree/main/spell-check-dictionary&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Specification* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12590"&gt;https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12590&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Design docs* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/tree/main/spell-check-dictionary"&gt;https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/tree/main/spell-check-dictionary&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ND1a1Z4i6kXMHqMwEyRkHSj5VVTWgX5Ya0aNLgVQYGw/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.kmfizh6cwyy4"&gt;https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ND1a1Z4i6kXMHqMwEyRkHSj5VVTWgX5Ya0aNLgVQYGw/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.kmfizh6cwyy4&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Summary* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Adds document.spellCheckCustomDictionary, which lets a page add words &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; to and remove words from a per-document dictionary that the browser's &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; spell &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; checker consults alongside its built-in dictionaries. Matching words are &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; no &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; longer flagged as misspelled in that document, so sites with &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; domain-specific vocabulary, such as product names, technical terms or &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; proper nouns, can suppress false positives without asking users to edit &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; their browser dictionary. The words last only for the document's lifetime &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and cannot be read back. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Blink component* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Blink&gt;Editing&gt;Spellcheck &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3EEditing%3ESpellcheck%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3EEditing%3ESpellcheck%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; spell-check-custom-dictionary &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/spell-check-custom-dictionary"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/spell-check-custom-dictionary&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Motivation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Spell checkers routinely flag words that are correct within a &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; particular site but unknown to general dictionaries. Examples include: - A &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Pokémon wiki containing names like Pikachu or Charmander. - A financial &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; analysis dashboard referencing company‑specific product names or tickers. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; - &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; A medical or scientific tool using specialized terminology. False &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; positives &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; in these contexts are distracting, misleading, and erode user trust. While &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; browsers allow users to add custom words via browser settings panel, there &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; is currently no way for pages to provide a custom dictionary &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; programmatically that applies only within their own context in order to &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; reduce those false positives. Authors need a way to treat domain‑specific &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; words as “known” without requiring user intervention. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Initial public proposal* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/tree/main/spell-check-dictionary"&gt;https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/tree/main/spell-check-dictionary&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Search tags* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; spellcheck &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="http:///features#tags:spellcheck"&gt;http:///features#tags:spellcheck&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, spelling &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="http:///features#tags:spelling"&gt;http:///features#tags:spelling&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, custom dictionary &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="http:///features#tags:custom%20dictionary"&gt;http:///features#tags:custom%20dictionary&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, spellCheckCustomDictionary &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="http:///features#tags:spellCheckCustomDictionary"&gt;http:///features#tags:spellCheckCustomDictionary&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, addWords &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="http:///features#tags:addWords"&gt;http:///features#tags:addWords&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, removeWords &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="http:///features#tags:removeWords"&gt;http:///features#tags:removeWords&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1191"&gt;https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1191&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review status* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Issues addressed &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Risks* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This feature is purely additive. It doesn't change existing spell-check &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; behaviour, so the compatibility risk to existing content is minimal. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Interoperability risk is low severity - the only difference the user sees &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; is red squiggles under the custom words if the feature is absent. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Gecko*: Neutral ( &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1384"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1384&lt;/a&gt;) &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1384#issuecomment-5997330337"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1384#issuecomment-5997330337&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebKit*: Positive ( &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646&lt;/a&gt;) &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646#issuecomment-6054915329"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646#issuecomment-6054915329&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web developers*: Positive - Bloomberg requested this feature and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; sponsors Igalia's work on it. - Related demand elsewhere: * &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; whatwg/dom#547: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; a developer with a canvas-based editor asks to add page-specific words to &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the browser spellchecker: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/547#issuecomment-3969379684"&gt;https://github.com/whatwg/dom/issues/547#issuecomment-3969379684&lt;/a&gt; * &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Electron added session.addWordToSpellCheckerDictionary() so apps can add &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; custom words: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/api/session#sesaddwordtospellcheckerdictionaryword"&gt;https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/api/session#sesaddwordtospellcheckerdictionaryword&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Other signals*: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Ergonomics* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The API is a minimal write-only pair (addWords/removeWords) taking &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; string sequences, batched to match caller intent. It composes cleanly with &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; existing spellcheck; no interaction with other high-usage APIs. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Deliberately no read API, to keep the dictionary unobservable. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Activation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The API should be easy to adopt: one call and no setup is needed. It is &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; not polyfillable. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Security* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The API holds transient per-document state. There is no network &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; exposure, and it neither reads nor modifies OS or browser dictionaries. It &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; is per-frame based. the word set is owned by a single Document. Every &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; frame &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; gets its own independent set. The set is discarded when the document goes &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; away. Input is validated with empty/whitespace-padded/ill-formed-UTF-16 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; words rejected and size-capped to prevent resource abuse. The API is &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; write-only by design. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebView application risks* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; that it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None expected. This feature is purely additive. It adds a new interface &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and a new Document attribute, doesn't change or deprecate any existing &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; API, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and doesn't change spell-check behaviour for pages that don't call it. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Existing WebView apps are unaffected. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Debuggability* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The API is write-only to page script by design: JavaScript can't read &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the dictionary back, so it can't be used to probe the user's spell-check &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; state. Developers and tests can still see what a page has added: - &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DevTools &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; UI : Application &gt; Frames &gt; [frame] &gt; Frame details has a &quot;Spell check &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; custom dictionary&quot; section listing the words that frame's document has &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; added. - CDP : the experimental command &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Page.getSpellCheckCustomDictionary({frameId}) returns a frame's words in &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; sorted order. The DevTools UI uses it, and automation tools can call it &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; directly. It isn't available to page script. - Automated testing: the web &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; test mock spellchecker supports the API, so tests can check that added &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; words are no longer marked as misspelled. Following the DevTools console &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; policy, the API logs no console messages. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Mac, Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Supported on Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS, Android and Android &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebView, with the known limitations on Android and WebView listed below. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Not available on iOS. The API (document.spellCheckCustomDictionary, secure &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; contexts only) and the word set are implemented entirely in Blink. Custom &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; words are applied as a renderer-side filter over the spell-check results &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the renderer receives, so the same filter works with every spell-check &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; backend: Hunspell on Linux and ChromeOS, NSSpellChecker on macOS, the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Windows spell checker, and the Android platform spell checker. Any &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; differences between platforms come only from what the underlying spell &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; checker reports. Known limitations: - Android and Android WebView: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; misspellings surfaced by the IME/keyboard during composition are drawn as &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; IME suggestion markers and never travel through the spell-check result &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; path, so this API cannot suppress them. Misspellings reported through the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; browser's spell-check pipeline are suppressed correctly. - Android &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebView: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebView disables spell checking by default (AwSettings), so there is &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; nothing to check — and nothing to suppress — until the app or page opts in &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; (e.g. spellcheck=&quot;true&quot;). This is pre-existing WebView behaviour rather &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; than a limitation of this API; with spell checking enabled, behaviour &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; matches Android. - iOS: Chrome on iOS is built on WebKit and does not run &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Blink, so the API is not exposed. Windows, macOS, Linux and ChromeOS are &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; fully supported. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; There are no WPTs yet. The tests for the API surface are currently in &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Chromium's wpt_internal, which is Chromium-only. We plan to upstream the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; API-surface tests to WPT alongside the spec PR. The API's only effect is &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; whether a word gets a spelling marker. Spelling markers depend on the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; platform spellchecker and aren't observable from script, so there's no &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; portable way for a WPT to assert them. Existing spellcheck features (e.g. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the spellcheck attribute) have the same limitation. Chromium-side coverage &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; of that effect: - Web tests that read spelling markers through the mock &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; spellchecker. - Blink unit tests for input validation and word limits. - &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; components/spellcheck for applying the filter to the hybrid, platform and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; spelling-service results, lifetime across navigation, and the UMA metric. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; - &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Manual tests in wpt_internal/spell-check-custom-dictionary/ for checking &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the squiggles visually on each platform, including WebView. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *DevTrial instructions* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/blob/main/spell-check-dictionary/DevTrial_instructions.md"&gt;https://github.com/Igalia/explainers/blob/main/spell-check-dictionary/DevTrial_instructions.md&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Flag name on about://flags* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *No information provided* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Finch feature name* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; SpellCheckCustomDictionaryAPI &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Rollout plan* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Will ship enabled for all users &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; False &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Tracking bug* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/428005649"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/428005649&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Availability expectation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; At launch the API will be available in Chromium-based browsers on &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Windows, macOS, Linux, ChromeOS, Android and Android WebView. It will not &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; be available in Chrome on iOS, which uses WebKit. Mozilla's position is &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; neutral (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1384"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1384&lt;/a&gt;). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebKit engineers have reviewed the proposal and reported no major issues( &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/646&lt;/a&gt;). Neither &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; engine has announced implementation plans. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Adoption expectation* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This is a feature requested by a client. we'd expect it will be used &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; pretty soon once launched. Use scenarios include sites and editors with &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; domain-specific vocabulary, such as wikis, technical documentation, CMS &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; rich-text editors, and medical or financial tools, to suppress &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; false-positive misspelling marks. Because the feature isn't needed for &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; things to work (without it, users just see extra squiggles), we expect &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; sites to adopt it gradually through feature detection rather than all at &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; once. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Adoption plan* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; - MDN documentation (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/568348294"&gt;https://issuetracker.google.com/issues/568348294&lt;/a&gt;) &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; - The explainer, DevTrial instructions, and a demo page. - Tooling: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DevTools shows a frame's custom words in Application &gt; Frames &gt; Frame &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; details, and the experimental CDP command &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Page.getSpellCheckCustomDictionary makes them available to automated &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; tests. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; - Continuing the WHATWG and standards-position discussions with Mozilla &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; and &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebKit to improve availability across browsers &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Non-OSS dependencies* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Does the feature depend on any code or APIs outside the Chromium open &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; source repository and its open-source dependencies to function? &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; No. The word set and the suppression filter are entirely in &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Blink/Chromium. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Estimated milestones* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on desktop 159 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DevTrial on desktop 153 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on Android 159 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on WebView 159 &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Anticipated spec changes* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; of &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The spec PR (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12590"&gt;https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12590&lt;/a&gt;) is under &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; review at WHATWG and the proposal is at Stage 2 ( &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/12388"&gt;https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/12388&lt;/a&gt;). We don't expect changes &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; to the name or shape of the shipped API. The open feedback comes from the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; W3C i18n WG review (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/i18n-actions/issues/222"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/i18n-actions/issues/222&lt;/a&gt;). &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; It raises two areas including language metadata and matching rules that &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; may &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; lead to later, backward-compatible changes. In both cases the &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; compatibility &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; risk is low: changes would be additive or would only reduce false-positive &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; spelling marks. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/6185007701557248?gate=4929988981424128"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/6185007701557248?gate=4929988981424128&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Links to previous Intent discussions* &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Intent to Prototype: &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/687a1d04.170a0220.2dad83.0168.GAE%40google.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/687a1d04.170a0220.2dad83.0168.GAE%40google.com&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com"&gt;https://chromestatus.com&lt;/a&gt;&gt;. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; -- &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Groups &quot;blink-dev&quot; group. &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; an email to &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="30525c595e5b1d5455461b1e1e1e705358425f5d59455d1e5f4257"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;. &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; To view this discussion visit &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6acf54ea.6e0b2b3a.151e33.12ef.GAE%40google.com"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6acf54ea.6e0b2b3a.151e33.12ef.GAE%40google.com&lt;/a&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6acf54ea.6e0b2b3a.151e33.12ef.GAE%40google.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer"&gt;https://groups.google.com/a/chromium.org/d/msgid/blink-dev/6acf54ea.6e0b2b3a.151e33.12ef.GAE%40google.com?utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=footer&lt;/a&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; . &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;
